# SRG-APP-000224-WSR-000138  -  CAT II
# The web server must generate a session ID using as much of the character
# set as possible to reduce the risk of brute force.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000224-WSR-000138
group:           V-206402
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001188
control_rev5:    SC-23

contribution:    The character set used to compose a session identifier is chosen by Splunk
                 Enterprise's session subsystem. The product offers no setting governing
                 which characters appear in a generated identifier, so there is nothing for
                 the application to configure toward this requirement.

external:        The character set used in generated session identifiers - a fixed property
                 of Splunk Enterprise's session subsystem; establish it by inspecting an
                 issued session cookie or by vendor attestation from Splunk

manual_reason:   No setting in web.conf or any other configuration file governs the
                 alphabet used for session identifier generation; the tools.sessions.*
                 settings control only session support, expiry, storage and cookie
                 attributes. A condition over any of them would not decide the requirement.

citation:        web.conf.spec:920-983 (the complete set of tools.sessions.* settings; none
                 specifies a character set or alphabet for generated identifiers)
finding:         Not decidable from Splunk configuration: the product exposes no setting
                 for the session identifier character set, so the application's share of
                 this requirement is satisfied by product design and the character set must
                 be established by inspecting an issued cookie or by vendor attestation.
